        {
          "secNumber": "3.6",
          "heading": "Quantiles and inverse distribution functions",
          "content": "For a probability level $u$ strictly between zero and one, a standard quantile convention is\n\n$$\nQ(u)=\\inf\\{x:F_X(x)\\geq u\\}.\n$$\n\nThis generalized inverse works even when the CDF has jumps or flat portions. In a strictly increasing continuous part, solving $F_X(x)=u$ produces the same result. A discrete quantile need not have cumulative probability exactly equal to the requested level. It is the first value at which the cumulative probability reaches or exceeds that level.\n\nThe median is a central quantile, often specified by the same generalized-inverse convention at one half. Other median definitions allow any value with at least half the probability at or below it and at least half at or above it. These conventions can yield an interval of acceptable medians in some discrete models. A calculation should identify its convention rather than labeling every different reported value an error.\n\nQuantiles and means answer different questions. A ninetieth percentile is a threshold exceeded with a specified tail probability under the chosen convention. A mean is an average defined through expectation, which will be developed in the next unit. A distribution can have a finite median while lacking a finite mean. Treating a central quantile as an average of values replaces one summary with another without justification.\n\nInverse-CDF sampling uses a uniform variable on the interval from zero to one and transforms it through the generalized inverse. The resulting variable has the target distribution under suitable endpoint conventions. The idea can be verified by relating threshold events for the inverse to the corresponding cumulative probability. A program implementation must still handle numerical endpoints, finite precision and noninvertible flat portions correctly. Calling a numerical routine inverse does not prove that its output follows the desired law.\n\nFor a discrete PMF, inverse sampling divides the uniform interval into subintervals whose lengths equal the masses. A uniform value selects the first cumulative threshold it does not exceed, according to the stated convention. For an absolutely continuous CDF with an explicit inverse, the transformation can be direct. When no convenient inverse is available, numerical inversion or another sampling algorithm may be preferable. The sampling method is a representation of the distribution, not part of its definition."
        },
        {
          "secNumber": "3.7",
          "heading": "Transformations through events and distribution functions",
          "content": "If $Y=g(X)$, a reliable starting point is the event identity $\\{Y\\leq y\\}=\\{g(X)\\leq y\\}$. The CDF of $Y$ is the probability of that event under the known law of $X$. This method works for discrete, continuous and mixed variables and handles many-to-one transformations naturally. It should be preferred whenever a remembered density formula would hide the transformed support or miss a branch.\n\nFor a strictly increasing transformation, the event becomes $\\{X\\leq g^{-1}(y)\\}$ on the transformed range. For a strictly decreasing transformation, it becomes a lower-bound event for $X$, so the complement and possible endpoint mass must be handled. For an absolutely continuous variable with no endpoint atom, the resulting density can often be obtained by differentiating the transformed CDF. Deriving the CDF first makes the sign and range changes visible.\n\nFor a differentiable one-to-one transformation with nonzero derivative on the relevant range, the density rule is\n\n$$\nf_Y(y)=f_X(g^{-1}(y))\\left|\\frac{d}{dy}g^{-1}(y)\\right|.\n$$\n\nThe absolute value ensures nonnegative density for a decreasing transformation. It accounts for the stretching or compression of interval widths. If a small transformed interval corresponds to a larger original interval, its density must be larger in order to preserve the same probability area. This width adjustment has no analogue in relabeling discrete point masses.\n\nA linear transformation $Y=a+bX$ with nonzero $b$ has density $f_X((y-a)/b)/|b|$ when $X$ is absolutely continuous. The transformed support must also be changed. If $b$ is negative, the endpoints reverse order. A density copied onto the old support can have the correct-looking formula and still describe the wrong distribution. If $b=0$, the transformed variable is a point mass at $a$, so the ordinary density formula is inapplicable.\n\nFor a transformation with several monotone branches, each inverse branch contributes a density term where it lies in the original support. The total density is the sum of those terms. Squaring a variable often combines a positive and a negative branch, but an asymmetric original range can admit both branches for some transformed values and only one for others. A blanket factor of two is therefore not generally correct.\n\nThe branch formula assumes the regularity needed for differentiating and changing variables on the stated pieces. At isolated critical values where derivatives vanish or branches meet, density expressions may diverge or need separate limiting descriptions. This does not automatically invalidate the distribution. An integrable singularity can still yield total probability one and a continuous CDF. The third worked question gives a square-root singularity that is integrable and has no atom at zero.\n\nTransformations can also create atoms. If a continuous variable is mapped to zero for an entire interval, the probability of that interval becomes a point mass at zero. Rounding, thresholding and clipping are common examples. A function need not be one-to-one or smooth simply because its input is continuous. The event-based method accounts for those collapsed regions; a smooth inverse-density formula used indiscriminately would omit their probability."
        },
        {
          "secNumber": "3.8",
          "heading": "Rounding and clipping as probability transformations",
          "content": "Transforming a variable through a recording rule is different from transforming its physical units. A change from seconds to milliseconds is one-to-one and preserves all information in an exact value. Rounding a time to the nearest second combines many different physical values into one displayed integer. Clipping a sensor at a maximum value combines every sufficiently large physical reading into a single boundary value. These rules alter distribution type and should be modeled as functions of an underlying quantity.\n\nTo make the distinction concrete, consider a uniform underlying variable on the interval from zero to four. Its density is one quarter per original unit. If it is rounded to the nearest integer, with ties assigned by any fixed rule, the displayed zero corresponds to an underlying interval of length one half. The displayed four also corresponds to an interval of length one half. Each has probability one eighth. The displayed values one, two and three each correspond to intervals of length one and therefore each have probability one quarter. The displayed values are not equally likely even though they are consecutive integer labels.\n\nThe tie rule at exact half-integers does not affect these probabilities because the underlying model has no point masses there. If the underlying distribution did have an atom at a half-integer, the tie rule would matter. This qualification illustrates a broader principle: an endpoint detail that is irrelevant under one distribution can be consequential under another. Numerical software should record the actual rounding convention rather than assuming that all possible inputs follow an absolutely continuous law.\n\nNow apply clipping instead of rounding. Let the recorded variable equal the underlying uniform value when it is below three and equal three otherwise. Below three, the recorded distribution retains the original continuous density one quarter. At three, the probability of the entire underlying interval from three to four becomes an atom of one quarter. The continuous area is three quarters, and the atom supplies the remaining quarter. An analyst fitting a purely continuous density to the recorded values would miss the boundary pileup caused by the instrument.\n\nThe clipped CDF equals zero below zero, equals one quarter times the threshold from zero up to three, and jumps to one at three. It is constant thereafter. The difference between its left limit at three and its value at three is one quarter. This representation shows the atom directly and handles probabilities such as a reading of at least three without relying on an inappropriate density-at-the-boundary calculation.\n\nThese examples also distinguish a property of the measured phenomenon from a property of the recording process. A concentration of displayed readings at a sensor limit can arise from clipping even when the physical quantity has no concentration at that exact value. Repeated displayed values do not by themselves identify the underlying distribution type. A defensible analysis specifies the physical model, the recording map and the resulting observed-data model separately."
        },
        {
          "secNumber": "3.9",
          "heading": "Comparing distributions through their CDFs",
          "content": "A full CDF contains more information than a single central summary. If one variable has a smaller CDF than another at every threshold, it places less probability below every threshold and is larger in the sense of first-order stochastic dominance. This is a distributional comparison, not a claim that every individual realized value of the first variable will exceed every realized value of the second. A statement about a pair of realized values would require a joint model.\n\nThe distinction can be seen with two uniform laws on overlapping intervals. A uniform variable on the interval from one to three is larger in this CDF sense than a uniform variable on the interval from zero to two. At every threshold its accumulated probability is no greater. Yet a draw near one from the first law can be less than a draw near two from the second law. Dominance concerns the entire marginal distributions, while a pairwise comparison concerns how two values are generated together.\n\nCDFs can also cross. One distribution may put more probability below small thresholds but less below larger thresholds. In that case there is no simple ordering of all thresholds. Reporting only that one median is larger can conceal the crossing and the different tail behavior. A threshold-specific question, such as the chance of exceeding a capacity limit, should be answered using that threshold rather than inferred from a central summary alone.\n\nQuantile curves supply another view of the comparison. A generalized inverse maps a probability level to a threshold. When a CDF dominance order holds, the corresponding quantiles are ordered in the opposite threshold direction: the distribution that accumulates probability more slowly reaches a specified level at a larger value. For crossing CDFs, the quantile comparison can depend on the chosen level. This is why a ninetieth-percentile comparison need not agree with a median comparison.\n\nTwo distributions can share several quantiles and still differ elsewhere. Knowing the median and two tail quantiles does not establish identical PMFs or densities. Likewise, two plotted curves may look similar at a coarse resolution while differing materially in a narrow interval. The numerical event of interest should be checked directly with the distribution formulas or sufficiently accurate integration. Visual similarity is useful evidence about shape, but it does not replace probability calculations."
        },
        {
          "secNumber": "3.10",
          "heading": "Why generalized inverse sampling works",
          "content": "The generalized inverse has a probabilistic purpose beyond reporting quantiles. Let $U$ be uniform on the interval from zero to one and set $Y=Q(U)$ for a CDF $F$. Apart from endpoints of zero probability under this uniform law, the threshold relation between the inverse and the original CDF is $Q(U)\\leq x$ exactly when $U\\leq F(x)$. The probability of the latter event is $F(x)$. Therefore the generated variable has CDF $F$.\n\nFor a discrete law, this threshold argument is particularly transparent. Each jump in the CDF corresponds to an interval of uniform input values of the same length. All inputs in that interval map to the supported value at the jump. A large mass receives a wide input interval, while a small mass receives a narrow one. No interpolation between supported values is needed, and such interpolation would generally generate the wrong distribution.\n\nFor a mixed law, a jump similarly produces a flat segment in the quantile function. A whole interval of uniform inputs maps to the atomic value. On the continuous portion, the quantile changes continuously where the CDF is strictly increasing. Thus the same sampling principle handles both point masses and continuous probability without pretending that an atom is an ordinary density peak. The input interval assigned to the atom carries its probability exactly.\n\nNumerical endpoints require care. Some pseudorandom interfaces can return zero, while others exclude both endpoints or use a different interval convention. A theoretical inverse may be infinite at a boundary for an unbounded distribution. A robust implementation follows a documented input convention and checks the transformed output for validity. Silently replacing every nonfinite value by zero changes the distribution and creates an artificial atom. Numerical repair should preserve the intended probability law rather than merely produce a finite display.\n\nThe inverse method does not require observations from a physical phenomenon with the target law. It produces model-generated samples from uniform inputs. Those samples can illustrate a distribution and test an implementation, but they do not establish that the distribution describes real data. This limitation is especially important when simulations use attractive plots: successful sampling validates a computational mechanism conditional on the model, not the model's empirical suitability."
        },
        {
          "secNumber": "3.11",
          "heading": "Computational and conceptual checks",
          "content": "For a PMF, check nonnegative masses, an explicit support and a total of one. For a density, check nonnegativity and total area over the entire range. For a CDF, check monotonicity, right-continuity and the limits at both infinities. For a mixed model, account for each atom separately and verify that continuous and atomic weights sum to one. These requirements concern the mathematical law before any numerical display or simulation is considered.\n\nFor a transformation, first find the image of the original support. Then identify whether several original values produce the same transformed value. A discrete transformation collects masses; a continuous one-to-one transformation adjusts interval widths; a many-to-one continuous transformation sums valid branches; and a transformation that collapses intervals may create atoms. The event-based CDF method offers one coherent starting point for all of them.\n\nNumerical integration should be checked against exact integrals where available. Uniform grids can perform poorly near integrable singularities if they evaluate an infinite endpoint density directly. Transforming the integration variable, excluding endpoints appropriately, or using an adaptive rule can address that numerical issue. A program returning an invalid number at an endpoint does not prove that the probability model is invalid. The mathematical integral and the chosen numerical representation must be examined separately.\n\nAn educational density explorer should show the shaded area of a selected interval and the corresponding CDF difference. It should label a density ordinate with reciprocal units when the variable has units, and it should display atoms distinctly in mixed models. Controls changing interval endpoints must preserve their order or explain an invalid input. A plot alone cannot teach these distinctions if its output labels call both a curve height and a shaded area probability.\n\nFinally, compare every answer with its event. A tail probability must correspond to the chosen strict or nonstrict inequality. A quantile must use its stated convention. A transformed variable's value should not be interpreted as retaining information that the transformation discarded. These habits prepare the reader for joint distributions and expectation, where compact notation makes it even more important to keep the underlying events and assumptions visible."
        }
