          "content": "For a discrete value $y$ with $p_Y(y)>0$, the conditional PMF of $X$ given $Y=y$ is the corresponding joint column divided by its total:\n\n$$\np_{X\\mid Y}(x\\mid y)=\\frac{p_{X,Y}(x,y)}{p_Y(y)}.\n$$\n\nThe conditional masses sum to one within that column. Row conditioning follows the same principle with the labels exchanged. A table may contain a row or column of total zero. The elementary conditional distribution there is undefined, since the model provides no positive-probability reference collection to normalize. Returning an arbitrary uniform distribution in that case would invent information.\n\nFor a pair with a joint density, a conditional density can be specified for relevant values with positive marginal density by\n\n$$\nf_{X\\mid Y}(x\\mid y)=\\frac{f_{X,Y}(x,y)}{f_Y(y)}.\n$$\n\nThis formula is a conditional-density construction. It is not obtained by dividing the zero point probability of a pair by the zero point probability of one continuous coordinate. Its justification comes from the joint-density factorization and integration identities. Conditional densities are defined up to appropriate marginal-null sets; isolated values without marginal weight need not have a uniquely determined conditional law.\n\nA useful elementary interpretation considers a thin band of $Y$ values near a given value. Under regularity conditions, the distribution of $X$ within such bands approaches the conditional density. This provides intuition but is not a substitute for checking the density formula and its support. In particular, a denominator evaluated outside the marginal support cannot create a meaningful conditional law through a formal ratio.\n\nThe support of a conditional distribution can change with the conditioning value. If $0<X<Y<1$, then given an interior value of $Y$, the allowed $X$ values lie between zero and that value. A conditional density uniform on that changing interval does not imply that the unconditional $X$ distribution is uniform on the whole unit interval. Averaging across conditioning values reweights the overlapping intervals and produces a different margin.\n\nConditional distributions recover the joint distribution when combined with the conditioning margin. In the discrete case, multiply conditional masses by the margin. In the continuous case, multiply conditional densities by the marginal density where the factorization is defined. Averaging a conditional event probability over the conditioning law recovers the event's unconditional probability. This is the distributional form of the law of total probability.",
