          "content": "Two random variables are independent when their events factorize across all relevant sets. In terms of their CDFs, independence means $F_{X,Y}(x,y)=F_X(x)F_Y(y)$ for every pair of real thresholds. For discrete variables, factorization of the joint PMF into the two marginal PMFs at all pairs is equivalent. For a pair with densities, factorization of the joint density into marginal densities almost everywhere provides the corresponding criterion.\n\nChecking one joint cell does not establish independence. Checking one tail event does not establish it either. The criterion concerns the whole law. For a small finite table, every cell can be compared with the product of its row and column margins. For a parametric density, a symbolic factorization plus a correct support analysis can establish the property. A nonrectangular support often signals dependence when the marginal supports would permit pairs forbidden by the joint law.\n\nIf variables are independent, measurable functions of them remain independent. Events concerning the transformed variables correspond to events concerning the original variables, and the original factorization applies. This permits independent components to be rescaled, thresholded or otherwise transformed without creating cross-component dependence. It does not imply that two functions of the same random variable are independent. Sharing a common input can generate strong dependence.\n\nIndependence also justifies products of expectations under appropriate integrability conditions. If independent variables have finite absolute first moments, their product is integrable and its expectation factorizes. Without independence, factorization generally fails. Without the existence conditions, an expression involving undefined or infinite signed expectations may be meaningless. A formula should not be used simply because its algebra resembles multiplication of ordinary numbers."
