          "content": "Conditional expectation obeys linearity within a fixed information set. A function of the conditioning variable is known under that information, so it can be taken outside a conditional average when the expressions are integrable. In particular, $E[h(Y)X\\mid Y]=h(Y)E[X\\mid Y]$. This identity supports the zero-cross-term arguments used for total variance and squared-error prediction. Its interpretation is straightforward: a quantity already determined by the information is a constant during averaging over the remaining uncertainty.\n\nNonlinear functions do not generally commute with expectation. The conditional expectation of a square is not the square of the conditional expectation. Their difference is the conditional variance: $E[X^2\\mid Y]-(E[X\\mid Y])^2=\\operatorname{Var}(X\\mid Y)$. This quantity is nonnegative, showing directly why squaring an average usually understates the average squared value. Equality requires no residual variation under the given information, up to sets of probability zero.\n\nThe same distinction applies to unconditional averages. A population with equal probabilities on zero and two has mean one and second moment two. Squaring the mean gives one rather than two. The missing difference is variance. This simple calculation is a useful diagnostic when a nonlinear prediction is produced by applying a function to an average input. Such a prediction may differ from the average outcome under the full input distribution.\n\nThe total-covariance law can be derived without memorizing a separate formula. Write each centered variable as its residual after conditioning plus its conditional mean minus the overall mean. Multiply the two decompositions. Averaging the residual product within the information set gives conditional covariance. Each mixed residual-and-conditional-mean term has expectation zero because the residual has conditional mean zero. The final mean-product term is the covariance of the conditional means.\n\nThis derivation also reveals why conditional independence is sufficient but not necessary for the residual covariance term to vanish. Conditional uncorrelatedness can eliminate that term even when the residuals have a nonlinear dependence. Conversely, conditional independence cannot eliminate covariance among the conditional means. Distinguishing the two terms prevents a common mistake in mixture models: declaring outputs independent after checking only their within-group mechanisms.\n\nConditional covariance can have different signs across groups. Their weighted average might cancel, while the between-mean covariance remains. A zero overall covariance can therefore arise through several mechanisms and does not identify one unique dependence structure. Examining only the final scalar summary discards information contained in conditional distributions. A joint-law or stratified analysis may be needed to understand the relationship relevant to a practical decision.\n\nThe variance of the conditional mean cannot exceed total variance because the remaining term is nonnegative. It equals total variance only when the conditional variance averages to zero. For a nonnegative conditional variance, a zero average implies it is zero almost surely. In that limiting case the information determines the variable up to model-null sets. At the opposite extreme, a constant conditional mean yields zero between-mean variance, though other kinds of dependence on the information can still remain through conditional spread or shape."
