          "secNumber": "6.8",
          "heading": "Mean and finite-population variance",
          "content": "Represent the marked count as the sum of indicators for the sample's sequential draw positions. Each position has marginal marked probability $p=K/N$ under uniform drawing without replacement. Thus the mean is $np=nK/N$. Although the margins match a binomial sequence with this probability, the positions are dependent because a draw changes the remaining collection.\n\nFor two different draw positions when $N>1$, the probability that both are marked is $K(K-1)/(N(N-1))$. Subtracting the product of their marginal probabilities gives covariance $-p(1-p)/(N-1)$. This is nonpositive and strictly negative for an interior population proportion. A marked draw removes one marked object, making another marked draw less likely than it would be under independent replacement sampling.\n\nExpanding variance of the indicator sum then gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Var}(X)=np(1-p)\\frac{N-n}{N-1},\\qquad N>1.\n$$\n\nThe ratio is the finite-population correction for the count variance under this parameter convention. It reduces the independent-trial variance because the covariance terms are negative. For a one-object collection, every valid fixed-size sample count is deterministic and its variance is zero; the displayed ratio should not be evaluated with a zero denominator.\n\nThe limiting cases help explain the formula. A sample of one has the Bernoulli variance for a uniform single draw. A census sample of all objects has a fixed marked count equal to $K$ and therefore zero variance. For a small sampling fraction in a large collection, the correction is close to one, so the binomial variance can be a useful approximation. For a substantial sampling fraction, ignoring the correction can markedly overstate sampling variation under this model.\n\nThe correction applies to this uniform finite-population design. It is not a universal reduction factor for every survey or dependent sequence. Cluster selection, unequal object probabilities, nonresponse and a random population composition can alter the joint law. Using a familiar finite-population formula without the corresponding mechanism can be just as misleading as imposing independence incorrectly."
