          "content": "With replacement and independent randomization, repeated uniform draws from a fixed collection have a constant marked probability and allow the same labeled object to be drawn again. The marked count is binomial. Without replacement, repeated labels are impossible and the marked proportion changes along the path, giving a hypergeometric count under uniform draws. Identical first-draw probabilities do not make the two full experiments equivalent.\n\nTheir means agree when the trial count and initial marked fraction agree. Their variances differ by the finite-population correction, and their supports can differ. A binomial model permits every count from zero to the trial count for an interior probability. A finite collection may prohibit some of those counts because too few marked or unmarked objects exist. An approximation assigning a small positive probability to an impossible count should be recognized as an approximation, not described as exact.\n\nFor fixed sample size and increasing population size with marked fraction approaching a fixed probability, the hypergeometric PMF approaches the binomial PMF. Successive without-replacement fractions change by increasingly small amounts along any fixed-length path. This gives an intuitive and algebraic basis for the approximation. If the sample size grows as a substantial proportion of the population, that reasoning no longer makes the same independence simplification, and the variance correction remains relevant.\n\nAn approximation rule based only on a sampling-fraction cutoff is a practical heuristic, not an error guarantee for every event. The accuracy required depends on the target tail or count probability. When exact hypergeometric computation is available and numerically stable, it is usually preferable for a genuinely finite uniform sample. An approximation can still teach the relationship between mechanisms, provided its discrepancy is measured and reported for the question at hand.",
