          "content": "A shared uncertain success probability produces a different count model from a binomial law with a fixed average probability. Let a variable $P$ represent that shared condition, and suppose the count is conditionally binomial given it, with fixed trial count $n$. Write $q=E[P]$ and $v=\\operatorname{Var}(P)$. Iterated expectation gives count mean $nq$. Total variance reveals the additional variation produced by the shared condition.\n\nThe average conditional variance is $nE[P(1-P)]$. Since $E[P^2]=q^2+v$, this term is $n(q(1-q)-v)$. The conditional mean is $nP$, whose variance is $n^2v$. Adding the two components gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Var}(X)=nq(1-q)+n(n-1)v.\n$$\n\nFor more than one trial and positive condition variance, the marginal count variance exceeds that of a fixed-probability binomial law with the same mean. This is one mechanism for overdispersion relative to that reference law. The word describes a comparison, not an explanation by itself. Dependence or heterogeneity must be modeled to determine where the extra variation comes from.\n\nFor example, suppose the shared probability is one fifth or four fifths with equal weights and the fixed count length is ten. Its mean probability is one half, and its variance is nine hundredths. The count mean is five, but its variance is $10(1/2)(1/2)+10(9)(0.09)=10.6$. A fixed-probability binomial model with mean five would have variance $2.5$. Replacing the uncertain shared condition by its average preserves the mean and discards most of the modeled variation.\n\nThis mixture differs from ten independent trials each having an independently redrawn uncertain probability with the same marginal mean. With independent condition draws at every position, marginal outcomes can again be independent binary trials with probability $q$. A single shared condition and separate independent conditions are different joint mechanisms. The phrase probability varies does not identify which mechanism is intended, so the timing and sharing of the variation must be stated.\n\nFinite-population sampling supplies a contrasting dependence structure. Without replacement from a fixed marked collection, covariance is negative and count variance is reduced. A shared uncertain condition can create positive covariance and increase variance. Neither smaller nor larger variance is a universal signature of the word random. The structure of the experiment determines the covariance terms and the resulting distribution."
