          "content": "The hypergeometric mean can also be derived using indicators indexed by population objects instead of draw positions. For each marked object, define an indicator that it is included in the uniform sample. A fixed object has inclusion probability $n/N$ because it occurs in the same number of equally weighted subsets as every other object. Summing the inclusion indicators over the marked set gives the marked sample count and mean $Kn/N$.\n\nTwo distinct population objects are both included with probability $n(n-1)/(N(N-1))$ for $N>1$. Subtracting the product of their inclusion probabilities gives a negative inclusion covariance. Its form differs from the draw-position covariance because the indicators describe different events: inclusion of fixed objects rather than category results at fixed positions. Both representations yield the same count variance when the correct number of indicators and pair terms are used.\n\nFor a positive sample size, the sample marked proportion is the count divided by $n$. Its design variance is the count variance divided by $n^2$. The factor $1/n$ reflects averaging, while the finite-population correction reflects depletion without replacement. If all objects are selected, the sample proportion equals the fixed population proportion exactly and its sampling variance is zero. This conclusion concerns selection variability in the fixed finite model, not every possible source of measurement error.\n\nAnother common notation uses the finite-population variance of the zero-one marks with denominator $N-1$. That quantity is $S^2=Np(1-p)/(N-1)$. The sample-proportion variance can then be written $(1-n/N)S^2/n$. This expression is algebraically identical to the earlier count-derived result. Different variance-denominator conventions can make two correct formulas look different; converting them to a shared convention prevents a false disagreement.\n\nThe denominator $N-1$ in this finite-population summary should not be confused with estimating an unknown variance from a particular realized sample. Here the entire fixed population of marks defines the quantity. The sampling variance formula follows from the uniform selection design. Its interpretation does not depend on pretending that the population marks are independent random draws from a superpopulation.\n\nIf a separate model makes the population marks independently random with a common probability before sampling, unconditional predictions can differ from predictions conditional on the realized marked total. After conditioning on that total, the selected count is hypergeometric. Without conditioning on it, a uniformly selected collection of distinct independently marked objects can have a binomial count. The distinction illustrates once more that conditioning and the level of modeled randomness determine the law."
